  "source": {
    "source_id": "arxiv2610.20492v1-ActualCosinePrecisionAdmissibility",
    "anchor": "S4.E11 literal hard precision; S4.Thmtheorem6 Lemma4.6; AppendixC.4 A3.SS4 selected matrix admissibility and actual Gaussian target context.",
    "original_text": "Every actual cosine-grid precision lies between the identity and κ times the identity and is positive definite. I_d\\preceq A\\preceq\\kappa I_d,\\qquad A\\succ0. \\(d\\in\\mathbb N,\\ d\\ge2,\\ \\kappa\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\kappa\\ge2\\). Fix any actual \\(O\\in O(d)\\), including either determinant sign. The assertion holds pointwise; no Haar law or private-randomness premise is needed. For source indices \\(i=1,\\ldots,d\\), let \\(\\theta_i=(i-\\tfrac12)\\pi/d\\), \\(\\lambda_i=(\\kappa+1)/2+(\\kappa-1)\\cos\\theta_i/2\\), and \\(A=O\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda_i)O^\\top\\). Lean uses zero-based \\(i.\\mathrm{val}+1/2\\); the focused Test proves that index alignment. Matrix order is quadratic-form order: \\(I_d\\preceq A\\) means \\(A-I_d\\) is positive semidefinite, and \\(A\\preceq\\kappa I_d\\) means \\(\\kappa I_d-A\\) is positive semidefinite. This does not require every entry to be nonnegative. The finite-support definition covers all real coordinate vectors. Positive definiteness, symmetry, scalar interval bounds and both orthogonality identities are produced or obtained from the source orthogonal-group input; none is a new public hypothesis. The dimension premise is retained exactly even though the cosine envelope works for every angle. The actual Gaussian consumer is \\(\\pi_A=N(0,A^{-1})\\) on Euclidean space with Lebesgue reference volume and real exponent \\(d/2\\), including odd dimensions. Its positive precision comes from this producer. Known-normalizer products independent of \\(O\\), full Appendix C.4 calculus/minimizer, moments/Krylov, adaptive/precision/cost/TV adapters and both complete main bounds remain separate.",
    "text_sha256": "a5b257be21431f91baaf2309ca42bc33064a13cefc7269895cc2dc37b9b1f9ba"
  },
  "lean": {
    "declaration": "AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.GaussianComplexity.FixedSpectrumAdmissibility.actual_precision_admissible",
    "file": "AutoSamplingTheory/ExampleCases/GaussianComplexity/FixedSpectrumAdmissibility.lean",
    "statement": "∀ {d : ℕ} (hd : 2 ≤ d)\n    {κ : ℝ} (hκ : 2 ≤ κ)\n    (O : Matrix.orthogonalGroup (Fin d) ℝ),\n    let θ : Fin d → ℝ := fun i => ((i.val : ℝ) + 1 / 2) * Real.pi / (d : ℝ)\n    let A : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℝ :=\n      (O : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℝ) *\n        Matrix.diagonal (fun i => (κ + 1) / 2 + (κ - 1) / 2 * Real.cos (θ i)) *\n        (O : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℝ).transpose\n    A.PosDef ∧ (A - 1).PosSemidef ∧\n      (κ • (1 : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) ℝ) - A).PosSemidef\n",
    "statement_sha256": "f2392fc64ec6ba741290ac66fe951fa67d819ec111a943446045782e2e38e319",
    "compiled": true
  },
  "blind_reconstruction": {
    "text": "For every natural number d with d >= 2, every real number kappa with kappa >= 2, and every real d-by-d orthogonal matrix O, define, for each index i in {0, 1, ..., d-1},\n    theta_i = (i + 1/2) pi / d,\n    lambda_i = (kappa + 1)/2 + ((kappa - 1)/2) cos(theta_i),\nand define\n    A = O diag(lambda_0, ..., lambda_(d-1)) O^T.\nHere O ranges over the full real orthogonal group: O O^T = O^T O = I_d, with either determinant sign permitted. The index i and the dimension d are cast to real numbers in the formula for theta_i. Every displayed division in these formulas is real division. In particular, 1/2 is the real number one-half, not a natural-number quotient; the exact literal grid is (i + 1/2) pi / d. The assumption d >= 2 ensures that its real denominator d is nonzero.\n\nThen A is symmetric positive definite, A - I_d is symmetric positive semidefinite, and kappa I_d - A is symmetric positive semidefinite, all at the same d, kappa, and O. Explicitly, A = A^T; for every nonzero x in R^d, x^T A x > 0; for every x in R^d, x^T (A - I_d) x >= 0 and x^T (kappa I_d - A) x >= 0. The two latter matrices are symmetric as part of their positive-semidefinite assertions. Equivalently, the bounds are I_d <= A <= kappa I_d in quadratic-form order: for every x in R^d, x^T x <= x^T A x <= kappa x^T x. These are matrix quadratic-form bounds, not comparisons of individual entries.\n\nAll real coordinate vectors are covered; finite support adds no restriction because the coordinate set is finite. Positivity is an asserted output for the constructed matrices, not an input assumption on A or on the diagonal values. The only input restrictions are d >= 2, kappa >= 2, and membership of O in the full orthogonal group. The statement makes no probabilistic, distributional, sampling, or algorithmic assertion about O. There are no existential or hidden constants: the displayed coefficients depend only on kappa and the literal numerical constants, theta_i depends only on i and d, and A depends on d, kappa, and O through the displayed construction. The assertions hold for every allowed triple (d, kappa, O), without any further hypotheses.\n",
    "text_sha256": "205efbc539703ab9e8cf408f24e9d3b56524ff428cf24bc1dc38b147a225c731",
    "decoder": "/root/decode_918537",
    "decoder_packet_sha256": "255d9aa7729ef4ab791199024074f21618ce2d1b3fa28648d8d29d2ab8729285",
